!deck: Math::Calculus
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Calculus Limit Continuity

!id: IHFp7aCyLb
!front: What is a continuous function?
!back:
\( f \) is **continuous at** \( c \) if
\[ \lim_{x \to c} f(x) = f(c), \]
which requires:

- \( f(c) \) is defined
- \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) \) exists and equals \( f(c) \)

\( f \) is a **continuous function** if this holds at every point of its domain.
~~~

!id: GGfqqaxYMg
!front: What is a continuous from the left/right function \( f \)  at \( c \)?
!back:
\( f \) is continuous from the left at \( c \) if:

- \( f(c) \) exists
- \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) = f(c) \)

\( f \) is continuous from the right at \( c \) if:

- \( f(c) \) exists
- \( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) = f(c) \)
!extra: Two-sided continuity at \( c \) is equivalent to being continuous from both the left and the right at \( c \).
~~~

!id: m0iyqsxvib
!front: What do the one-sided limits \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \) and \( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \) mean?
!back:
\( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \) is the value \( f(x) \) tends to as \( x \to c \) with \( x < c \).

\( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \) is the value \( f(x) \) tends to as \( x \to c \) with \( x > c \).
~~~

!id: QMkoDLY08a
!front: How does the two-sided limit \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) \) relate to the one-sided limits \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \) and \( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \)?
!back:
The two-sided limit exists (as a finite number) if and only if both one-sided limits:

- exist
- are finite
- are equal to each other

Its value is then their common value.
!extra: This equivalence is why one-sided limits are the natural tool for classifying discontinuities: whether they exist, are finite, and agree determines which kind of discontinuity occurs at \( c \).
~~~

!id: fzHFwExGKp
!front: Why is continuity of \( f \) on a closed interval \( [a,b] \) stated using one-sided continuity at the endpoints \( a \) and \( b \)?
!back: Outside \( [a,b] \), \( f \) is not defined, so a genuine two-sided limit at \( a \) or \( b \) cannot be formed. All that can be asked is that \( f \) behave well from the side that lies in the domain: continuous from the right at \( a \), and continuous from the left at \( b \).
!extra: This is precisely the hypothesis theorems like the IVT and EVT need at the endpoints of \( [a,b] \).
~~~

!id: ow7Ke5CWpV
!front: What characterizes a discontinuity of the **first kind**?
!back:
Both one-sided limits, \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \) and \( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \):

- exist
- are finite

regardless of whether they agree with each other or with \( f(c) \).
!related: Contrast with the second kind, where at least one one-sided limit fails to exist as a finite number.
~~~

!id: cxHfPbW878
!front: What makes a discontinuity at \( x = c \) **removable**?
!back:
Both one-sided limits:

- exist
- are finite
- agree: \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) = \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) = L \)

But either \( f(c) \) is undefined or \( f(c) \neq L \).

It is called removable because defining (or redefining) \( f(c) = L \) makes \( f \) continuous at \( c \), without changing \( f \) anywhere else.
!extra: Example: \( f(x) = \frac{x^2-1}{x-1} \) at \( x=1 \); the limit is \( 2 \), but \( f(1) \) is undefined. ![The line f(x) = (x^2-1)/(x-1), which simplifies to x+1, with an open circle marking the hole at (1, 2): the two-sided limit exists and equals 2, but f(1) is undefined](img/removable-discontinuity.svg)
~~~

!id: FRG4k16Qle
!front: What makes a discontinuity at \( x = c \) a **jump discontinuity**?
!back:
Both one-sided limits:

- exist
- are finite
- disagree: \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \neq \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \)
!extra: Unlike a removable discontinuity, no single choice of \( f(c) \) can make \( f \) continuous at \( c \) — the left and right values genuinely disagree. ![Two curve arms meeting at x = c, with an open circle at each one-sided limit value: the left arm ends at L-minus and the right arm begins at L-plus, a strictly higher value, showing the two one-sided limits disagree](img/jump-discontinuity.svg)
~~~

!id: sYzobmQo2P
!front: What characterizes a discontinuity of the **second kind** (an **essential discontinuity**)?
!back:
At least one of \( \lim_{x \to c^-} f(x) \) or \( \lim_{x \to c^+} f(x) \) fails to exist as a finite number:

- it does not exist at all, or
- it is infinite
!extra: This is the complement of the first-kind case: first kind requires both one-sided limits to exist and be finite, so any failure of that condition puts the discontinuity in the second kind.
~~~

!id: KdFIcEaHYN
!front: Why is the discontinuity of \( f(x) = 1/x \) at \( x = 0 \) essential rather than removable?
!back:
- \( \lim_{x \to 0^-} f(x) = -\infty \)
- \( \lim_{x \to 0^+} f(x) = +\infty \)

Neither one-sided limit is a finite number, so no value assigned to \( f(0) \) could make \( f \) continuous there.
!extra: This case is sometimes singled out as an **infinite discontinuity**, a special case of an essential discontinuity where the one-sided limit(s) are infinite rather than simply nonexistent. ![The graph of f(x) = 1/x with a vertical asymptote at x = 0: the right branch climbs to +infinity and the left branch dives to -infinity as x approaches 0](img/infinite-discontinuity.svg)
~~~

!id: SUWcHWUEHj
!front: Give an example of an essential discontinuity caused by oscillation rather than by an infinite limit, and explain why.
!back: \( f(x) = \sin(1/x) \) at \( x = 0 \): as \( x \to 0 \), \( 1/x \) grows without bound, so \( \sin(1/x) \) oscillates through every value in \( [-1,1] \) infinitely often. Neither one-sided limit exists, since \( f(x) \) never settles near a single value.
!extra:
This is called an **oscillating discontinuity**, a subtype of essential discontinuity.

![The graph of f(x) = sin(1/x) oscillating with ever-increasing frequency as x approaches 0 from the right, bounded within the band between y = 1 and y = -1](img/oscillating-discontinuity.svg)
~~~

!id: HafZTETOS6
!front: State the \( \varepsilon \)-\( \delta \) definition of continuity of \( f \) at \( c \).
!back:
\( f \) is continuous at \( c \) if for every \( \varepsilon>0 \) there exists \( \delta>0 \) such that for all \( x \) in the domain of \( f \),
\[ |x-c|<\delta \implies |f(x)-f(c)|<\varepsilon. \]
!extra: ![The band of half-width epsilon around f(c) and the neighborhood of half-width delta around c, showing the curve stays inside the band whenever x lies within delta of c](img/epsilon-delta-continuity.svg)
~~~

!id: 2311aqAnmI
!front: How does the \( \varepsilon \)-\( \delta \) definition of continuity relate to the limit-based definition (\( f(c) \) exists, \( \lim_{x\to c} f(x) \) exists, and equals \( f(c) \))?
!back:
The two definitions express the same condition, at different levels of formality:

- The \( \varepsilon \)-\( \delta \) definition is the rigorous, formal way to express what it means for the limit to equal \( f(c) \).
- The limit-based definition is the more intuitive statement of the same idea: the function value at \( c \) must equal the limit of the function as \( x \to c \).
~~~

!id: ClxV6ZNLSX
!front: What does it mean for a function \( f \) to be piecewise continuous on a closed interval \( [a,b] \)?
!back:
\( f \) is piecewise continuous on \( [a,b] \) if:

- it has at most finitely many discontinuities on \( [a,b] \)
- each discontinuity is of the first kind (removable or jump)

Equivalently, no essential discontinuity (infinite or oscillating) is permitted.
~~~
